    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most homeowners do not need a $10,000 mower. They need a residential zero turn with the right deck for their lawn, a transmission sized for their acreage and a seat they can sit on for an hour. In 2026 a 10-gauge fabricated deck costs only $70 more than a stamped one, and that changes which machine is the smart buy.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-residential-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Residential Means for a Zero Turn
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built for One to Four Acres</h3>

          <p>
            Residential zero turn mowers are built for a homeowner who mows once a week from spring to fall, on a property of roughly one to four acres. That works out to somewhere between 30 and 120 hours a year. Every design choice in this class, from the deck steel to the transmission, assumes that duty cycle, and that is what separates them from the prosumer and commercial machines covered in our <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">main zero turn mower guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Big Differences Within the Class</h3>

          <p>
            Within that range there is a lot of difference. A 42 inch machine with a stamped deck and sealed transmissions suits a flat acre perfectly and will be strained on three acres of rough ground. A 54 or 60 inch machine with a fabricated deck and better transmissions handles four acres comfortably but is wasted, and may not even fit through the gate, on a half-acre suburban lot. Choosing well is mostly about matching the machine to your acreage, terrain and storage, not finding the most powerful mower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Four Specs to Read</h3>

          <p>
            There are four specs to read on any homeowner zero turn. The deck: stamped or fabricated, and what gauge of steel. The transmission: which Hydro-Gear or Tuff-Torq unit, and whether it is serviceable. The seat: plain, spring suspension or full suspension. And the warranty: years and hours, plus any lifetime coverage on the frame or deck shell. Engine horsepower matters less than most buyers think once you are above about 20 hp on a 42 inch deck.
          </p>

