        <div id="decks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Stamped vs Fabricated Decks at Homeowner Prices
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stamped vs Fabricated Construction</h3>

          <p>
            The deck is where residential mowers differ most, and where a few hundred dollars buys the most. A stamped deck is pressed from one sheet of steel, usually 11 to 13 gauge on residential mowers. It is light, cheap to make and perfectly adequate on smooth lawns. A fabricated deck is cut and welded from heavier plate, usually 10 gauge on homeowner machines, and it holds its shape after impacts and is easier to keep level over years of use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gauge Numbers Run Backwards</h3>

          <p>
            Gauge numbers run backwards, so lower is thicker. The Cub Cadet ZT1 42's 13-gauge stamped deck is the thinnest on this list. The Toro TimeCutter MyRIDE, both Husqvarnas, the John Deere Z330M and the Ariens Zenith all use 10-gauge decks, though Deere calls its Accel Deep deck forged and Toro calls its deck IronForged. The practical difference is how the deck survives hidden roots, edging stones and the occasional tree stump, which bend thin stamped decks and knock blades out of level.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fabricated Decks Now Cost Little More</h3>

          <p>
            The surprise in 2026 is how little a fabricated deck now costs. Husqvarna lists the Z242F Special Edition, with a 10-gauge fabricated 42 inch deck, at $3,499, just $70 more than the Cub Cadet ZT1 42's stamped-deck MSRP of $3,429. A few years ago that gap would have been closer to a thousand dollars. If your lawn has any roots, rocks or rough edges, take the fabricated deck.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck Depth, and When Stamped Is Still Right</h3>

          <p>
            Deck depth matters as much as thickness. A deeper deck gives grass room to stand up before the blade hits it and helps discharge wet clippings without clumping. Ariens lists the Zenith 60's ENVY Elite deck at 5.5 inches deep, which is why it handles thick spring grass better than shallower 42 inch decks. Stamped decks also tend to be shallower, so they are more likely to leave clumps when grass gets ahead of you.
          </p>

          <p>
            A stamped deck is still the right call on some lawns. On a flat, open, well-kept suburban acre with no obstacles, a stamped deck cuts as cleanly as a fabricated one at normal mowing heights, and Cub Cadet backs the ZT1's deck shell with a lifetime limited warranty. Just avoid mowing over roots and keep blades sharp.
          </p>

