        <div id="transmissions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. EZT, ZT-2100 and ZT-3100: Residential Transmissions Explained
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transmissions Divide the Price Tiers</h3>

          <p>
            A zero turn's two transmissions, one per rear wheel, are its most expensive part to replace and the clearest dividing line between price tiers. Nearly every residential mower uses Hydro-Gear units, and the model number tells you most of what you need to know about how long they will last and whether they can be serviced.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Entry Level: Hydro-Gear EZT</h3>

          <p>
            The entry level is Hydro-Gear's EZT family, including the EZT 2200 on the Cub Cadet ZT1 42 and the EZT units Deere lists on the Z330M. These are sealed integrated transmissions designed for residential hours. Cub Cadet's spec sheet lists the EZT 2200 as maintenance-free and not serviceable: there is no fluid change, and when one wears out, the whole unit is replaced. On a flat acre mowed weekly, that can take many seasons; on three acres of hills, it comes much sooner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step Up: ZT-2100 and ZT-2200</h3>

          <p>
            One step up is Hydro-Gear's ZT-2100 and ZT-2200 class, which Toro fits to the TimeCutter MyRIDE 42 as the HG-ZT2100. These are heavier-duty residential units, built for more load than the base EZTs, and a sensible upgrade for owners with slopes or larger lots. Husqvarna lists the Z242F and Z254FX only as Hydro-Gear hydrostatic, so ask the dealer which unit is fitted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Prosumer: ZT-3100 and ZT-3200, Plus Two Practical Points</h3>

          <p>
            The next class up, the ZT-3100 and ZT-3200, is where prosumer machines begin. These transaxles have serviceable fluid and are what retailer listings name for the Ariens Zenith 60. If you mow four acres or have steep slopes, a serviceable transaxle is the most valuable upgrade you can buy, because a fluid change at the interval in the manual extends its life considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical points. First, every transmission here is a hydrostatic drive, so the levers control speed smoothly with no gears to shift. Second, all of them run hotter and wear faster when pushed hard on hills, when towing heavy carts and in very hot weather. Keep the cooling fins clean, avoid long uphill runs with a loaded cart, and let the mower cool down before parking it in a closed shed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="acreage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Matching the Machine to 1, 2, 3 and 4 Acres
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Think in Hours per Season</h3>

          <p>
            Acreage is the starting point, but the real number to think about is hours per season, because that is what wears out residential transmissions and decks. A homeowner mowing weekly for about 30 weeks a year adds up hours fast on a larger lot, and a machine that is happy at 30 hours a year may be strained at 100.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">About One Acre</h3>

          <p>
            For about one acre, a 42 inch deck is the right size. The Cub Cadet ZT1 42, Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition and Toro TimeCutter MyRIDE 42 all fit, fit through standard garden gates and store in a typical shed. On a flat, open lot, the Cub Cadet is enough. If there are roots, beds and trees, take the fabricated decks on the Husqvarna or Toro.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">About Two Acres</h3>

          <p>
            At about two acres, a 42 inch machine still works but you will spend noticeably longer in the seat, and comfort starts to matter. The Toro's MyRIDE suspension and the Deere Z330M's 48 inch deck and spring suspension seat are the best fits. This is the size where the transmission upgrade on the Toro starts to pay for itself, particularly if the lot is not flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three to Four Acres and Beyond</h3>

          <p>
            At three to four acres, move to a 54 or 60 inch deck. The Husqvarna Z254FX's 54 inch fabricated deck and suspension seat suit smoother properties, while the Ariens Zenith 60's deeper deck and serviceable transaxles suit rougher ground and thicker grass. At this size the mowing time savings from a wider deck are significant, so long as your gates and storage can fit it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above about four acres, or if you mow more than about 120 hours a year, you are past what residential mowers are designed for. Look at prosumer machines in our main guide or the <a href="/best-commercial-zero-turn-mower/">best commercial zero turn mowers</a>, which carry commercial transaxles, heavier decks and warranties written in 1,000-plus hours. If your lot is under an acre, our guide to the <a href="/best-42-inch-zero-turn-mower/">best 42 inch zero turn mowers</a> goes deeper on the smallest size.
          </p>

