        <div id="hills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Residential Zero Turns on Hills
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Zero Turns Struggle on Hills</h3>

          <p>
            Hills are where residential zero turns are most limited, and where buyers most often get into trouble. A zero turn steers with its rear drive wheels, so when those wheels lose grip on a wet or steep slope, the mower loses both drive and steering at once. Residential machines, being lighter and on smaller tires than commercial mowers, lose grip sooner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Manual's Maximum Slope</h3>

          <p>
            Every operator's manual for these mowers includes a maximum slope and instructions to mow up and down slopes rather than across them. Read it before you buy, and measure your steepest section with a phone inclinometer app. If your lawn has steep banks, ditches or pond edges, a zero turn may be the wrong mower for those areas, and our guide to the <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/">best lawn mowers for hills</a> covers safer options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Specs That Help on Gentle Slopes</h3>

          <p>
            Within the residential class, a few specs help on gentle slopes. Wider rear tires, like the 20 by 10 tires Deere lists on the Z330M, grip better than narrower ones. A heavier machine with a lower center of gravity, such as the 60 inch Ariens, feels more planted. And stronger transmissions, like Toro's ZT-2100 or the ZT-3100 listed for the Ariens, handle the extra load of climbing better than base EZT units, which run hotter on long uphill passes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Technique Matters More Than Hardware</h3>

          <p>
            Technique matters more than hardware. Mow slopes when the grass is dry, slow down before turning, avoid sudden direction changes on a hill, and never mow near drop-offs. Keep the rollover protection bar up and the seat belt fastened if your mower has them. If a slope makes you uneasy on a zero turn, use a walk-behind or string trimmer there instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="comfort" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Seats and Suspension at Homeowner Prices
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Suspension Now Separates Similar Machines</h3>

          <p>
            Ride comfort used to be the thing you gave up to buy a residential zero turn. That has changed, and in 2026 suspension is one of the best ways to choose between machines at similar prices, especially if you mow two acres or more at a time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Basic Seats Without Suspension</h3>

          <p>
            There are three levels on this list. The basic level is a padded high-back seat without suspension, like the Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition's 15 inch vinyl seat and the Cub Cadet ZT1 42's 20 inch seat. They are fine for 45 minutes on a smooth lawn and tiring beyond that. The next level is a spring suspension seat, which Deere fits to the Z330M, and seat isolation dampers like the Ariens Zenith's, which take the edge off vibration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Suspended Platforms and Full Suspension Seats</h3>

          <p>
            The top level is a suspended operator platform or full suspension seat. Toro's MyRIDE system on the TimeCutter suspends the operator station, and the Husqvarna Z254FX has a full suspension seat with dial adjustment for operator weight. Both are rare at homeowner prices, and both make a real difference on rough or rolling lawns where every bump otherwise goes straight into your back.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sit on It Before You Buy</h3>

          <p>
            Before you buy, sit on the mower at the dealer or store with the levers in mowing position. Check that the seat slides far enough for the tallest person who will mow, that the levers adjust to your height, and that your feet rest comfortably on the platform. The Deere's 11-position levers with three height settings are a good example of what to look for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Each Extra $500 Buys
          </h2>

          <p>
            The residential zero turn market is unusually easy to compare on price, because the manufacturers publish MSRPs. Laid out in order, the picks on this page show exactly what each step up buys.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">$3,429 to $3,499: The Entry Machines</h3>

          <p>
            At $3,429 MSRP, the Cub Cadet Ultima ZT1 42 gets you a 42 inch stamped deck, a Kawasaki twin, sealed EZT 2200 transmissions and a strong warranty. At $3,499, the Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition swaps in a 10-gauge fabricated deck for $70 more, which is the best upgrade per dollar on this page. At $3,899, Deere's list price for the Z330M adds a 48 inch deck, a spring suspension seat and the Deere dealer network.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">$4,499 and Up: Suspension and Stronger Transmissions</h3>

          <p>
            At $4,499, the Toro TimeCutter MyRIDE 42 adds a suspension system and stronger ZT-2100 transmissions to a fabricated 42 inch deck, a combination that makes it our overall pick for one to two acres. At $4,699, the Husqvarna Z254FX moves to a 54 inch fabricated deck with a full suspension seat, the upgrade to make if your lot is three acres or more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Top of the List, and How to Shop</h3>

          <p>
            Above that, the Ariens Zenith 60 brings serviceable ZT-3100 transaxles per retailer listings, a 5.5 inch deep 60 inch deck and a 4-year or 750-hour warranty, at a price well above the rest of this list. It is only worth it on large or rough properties.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE BEST $70 YOU WILL SPEND</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A fabricated deck now costs almost nothing extra.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">At MSRP, the Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition's 10-gauge fabricated deck costs $70 more than the Cub Cadet ZT1 42's 13-gauge stamped deck. Unless your lawn is flat and obstacle-free, that is the upgrade to take first.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Dealer and seasonal pricing varies, and spring and fall promotions are common. Use MSRP to compare models, then shop for the best price on the one you have chosen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Where to Buy: Dealer, Big Box or Amazon
          </h2>

          <p>
            Residential zero turns are sold three ways, and the channel matters more than most buyers expect. Dealers sell Toro, Cub Cadet, John Deere, Husqvarna and Ariens; big-box stores carry some of the same brands, sometimes in store-specific models; and a few models, including the Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition, the Husqvarna Z254FX and the Ariens Zenith 60, are sold on Amazon.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buying From a Dealer</h3>

          <p>
            Buying from a dealer usually means delivery, setup and a pre-delivery inspection, plus a local shop for warranty work. Toro sells the TimeCutter MyRIDE 42 through dealers rather than online, and John Deere's ZTrak line goes through Deere dealers. That is not a drawback: a zero turn that needs a warranty repair in June is far easier to deal with if the shop that sold it will fix it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buying Online</h3>

          <p>
            Buying online can save money and time, but check what you are getting. Confirm the exact model number, since Husqvarna and Ariens use specific numbers for each configuration, and check how the mower is delivered, whether curbside freight or set up and ready to mow. Then find out where the nearest authorized service center is before you order, because warranty work still has to be done by one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whatever the Channel, Register and Record</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever the channel, register the warranty, keep the receipt, and record the hours and dates of oil changes and blade sharpening. Cub Cadet's 3-year unlimited-hour residential warranty and Toro's 3-year unlimited-hour residential warranty both assume normal homeowner use, and a service record helps if you ever need to make a claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Gates, Sheds and Storage
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Overall Width Is Wider Than the Deck</h3>

          <p>
            The most common regret with a residential zero turn is buying one that does not fit. Overall width is several inches wider than the deck because of the discharge chute and anti-scalp wheels, and many garden gates and shed doors are narrower than buyers assume.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Husqvarna's Published Widths</h3>

          <p>
            Husqvarna publishes useful numbers here. The Z242F Special Edition is 56 inches wide with the chute down and 48 inches with it up, so it needs at least a 48 inch opening, and only with the chute raised. The Z254FX is 67 inches wide with the chute down and 56 inches with it up, which rules out many standard gates entirely. Measure every gate, gap and shed door the mower will need to pass through before choosing a deck width.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Length, Weight and Ramps</h3>

          <p>
            Length and weight matter for storage too. The Husqvarnas are listed at 75 inches long, and weights on this list run from about 560 pounds for the Z242F to 643 for the Z254FX and 598 for the Deere. A soft lawn or a steep ramp into a shed can be a struggle for a machine that heavy, so plan a level, firm path.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Space Is Tight</h3>

          <p>
            If space is tight, a 42 inch machine is often the only sensible choice even on a larger lot, and our <a href="/best-42-inch-zero-turn-mower/">42 inch zero turn guide</a> compares more of them. Store the mower under cover, with a full tank of stabilized fuel or an empty carburetor over winter, and keep the battery on a maintainer so it starts in spring.
          </p>
        </div>

