        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best residential zero turn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners on one to two acres, the Toro TimeCutter MyRIDE 42 (75747) is our pick, combining a 10-gauge fabricated deck, Hydro-Gear ZT-2100 transmissions and MyRIDE suspension at a $4,499 MSRP. For two to four acres, the Husqvarna Z254FX's 54 inch fabricated deck and suspension seat are the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size zero turn does a homeowner need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 42 inch deck suits up to about two acres and fits most garden gates. A 48 inch deck suits two to two and a half acres, a 54 inch deck two to four acres, and a 60 inch deck three to four acres of open or rough ground. Check gate and shed widths before you decide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a fabricated deck worth it on a residential mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. A fabricated 10-gauge deck holds its shape after hitting roots and stones better than a 13-gauge stamped deck. In 2026 the price gap is small: the Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition's fabricated deck costs $70 more at MSRP than the Cub Cadet ZT1 42's stamped deck. On a flat, clear lawn, stamped is still fine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do residential zero turn mowers last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends mostly on hours and terrain. Residential warranties suggest the expected duty: Cub Cadet and Toro list 3-year unlimited-hour residential terms, and Ariens 4 years or 750 hours on the Zenith. Owners commonly report sealed EZT transmissions as the first major wear item on hilly or large lots.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are residential zero turns good on hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only on gentle slopes. They steer with their rear wheels, so losing grip means losing steering. Follow the slope limit in the operator's manual, mow up and down rather than across, and avoid wet grass. Stronger transmissions and wider rear tires help, but for steep banks a different mower is safer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you buy a residential zero turn mower on Amazon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some, yes. The Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition, Husqvarna Z254FX and Ariens Zenith 60 are sold on Amazon. Toro sells the TimeCutter MyRIDE 42 through dealers, and Cub Cadet and John Deere models go through dealers and stores. Check delivery terms and the nearest service center before ordering online.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a residential and commercial zero turn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Residential mowers use sealed or light-duty transmissions, 10 to 13 gauge decks and warranties of a few hundred hours, built for 30 to 120 hours a year. Commercial mowers use serviceable heavy-duty transaxles or pumps and wheel motors, 7-gauge decks and warranties written in 1,000-plus hours, at several times the price.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the lot you might have someday</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 60 inch mower on a one-acre lot is slower around trees, may not fit through the gate and costs thousands more. Buy for the property you mow today; a 42 or 48 inch machine covers one to two acres well and is easier to store.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking the transmission model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealed EZT units are fine for a flat acre but wear faster on hills and big lots. If you have slopes or more than two acres, pay for a ZT-2100 class or serviceable ZT-3100 transaxle; it is the part that is most expensive to replace.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing stamped to save $70</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At MSRP, the gap between the Cub Cadet ZT1 42's stamped deck and the Husqvarna Z242F's fabricated deck is $70. Unless your lawn is flat and free of roots and edging stones, the fabricated deck is worth more than that.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing slopes across instead of up and down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Residential zero turns are light and steer with their rear wheels, so they lose grip on side slopes. Follow the manual's slope limit, mow up and down, avoid wet grass, and use a trimmer or walk-behind on steep banks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not measuring the gate first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overall width runs several inches past the deck. The Husqvarna Z254FX is 56 inches wide even with the chute up. Measure every gate and shed door before you choose a deck size, not after the mower arrives.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

